# Review-Loop Guardrails (always on)

- Never claim a check passed unless you ran it in this session and saw
  the output. A check that did not run is reported as SKIPPED or
  UNVERIFIED, never as a pass.
- Never edit, delete, skip, or relax a test, assertion, lint rule, or
  type setting to make checks go green. If a test looks wrong, stop
  and report the evidence instead.
- A review finding needs file:line, a concrete failing scenario,
  expected vs. actual behavior, and reproduction or deterministic proof.
  Without that evidence, mark it UNVERIFIED and do not fix it.
- Fix one finding at a time with the smallest diff. Re-run the
  reproduction and the test/type/lint/build gate after each fix.
  Revert a fix that breaks a previously passing check.
- Where an invariant requires multiple database writes to succeed or
  fail together, use a transaction or equivalent atomic mechanism.
  Verify uniqueness, balance and referential invariants for the actual
  storage design; acknowledged durable writes must survive a restart.
- For payments, orders, and sends that may be retried, verify that
  retries, double clicks, and webhook redeliveries do not duplicate
  effects; use an idempotency key or equivalent mechanism as needed.
- Authorization is checked on the server for every access to
  user-owned data. Hiding a button is not authorization. Keep
  database row-level policies enabled where supported.
- Never hardcode or print secrets. Never log secrets or personal data.
- New dependencies must exist in the official registry and be pinned.
- Small changes only: no unrelated refactors or upgrades inside a fix.
- For a full review, run the `review-loop` skill instead of a free-form
  review: mechanical gate first, then lens reviews, reproduction
  before fixes, plain-language verdict at the end.
